Write each as `` `TOKEN` = gloss ``, and **end the gloss of a token that ASKS something with a
question mark** — that is how the optional Stop sensor tells one of the Owner's questions (which rule
1 answers in a line or two) from an instruction (which earns a full work report). The examples below
are fenced so they read as a format guide and the sensor does NOT mistake them for this Owner's
vocabulary; write your real rows OUTSIDE the fence.

```
`MIS` = make it so — proceed on the AGREED scope, no further check-in.
`AR` = archive ready? — is this thread closed out and verified on the remote.
```
The first is an INSTRUCTION (its gloss asks nothing); the second is a QUESTION (its gloss ends in
"?"). Copy that exact format — SINGLE backticks, exactly as shown; a double-backtick row will not
parse, and the sensor will warn that coverage is off. Treat the row shape as RESERVED NOTATION for
this file: a backticked ALL-CAPS token followed by "=" reads as a definition WHEREVER it appears in
this doc, so keep that shape out of unrelated prose — and out of your glosses: inside a gloss it
reads as the NEXT definition and ends the current one. Write your rows below, outside the fence.

